## Final Judgement
## Summary of Key Findings

The evidence confirms that Eric Trump, Donald J. Trump Jr., and Ivanka Trump were indeed barred from serving on charities in New York state as part of a November 2019 settlement with the New York Attorney General's office. However, the scope of the ban is limited to New York charities, not a blanket lifetime ban on "ever operating a charity." More critically, the evidence explicitly refutes the claim's stated reason for the ban: there is no mention of donations for children with cancer being stolen or misappropriated. The actual misconduct involved self-dealing, misuse of foundation funds for personal expenses (portraits, sports memorabilia), settling personal legal disputes, and making an impermissible political donation to Florida AG Pam Bondi. The evidence explicitly states the misconduct was not about "theft of donations earmarked for children with cancer."

## Missing Information

No additional information is needed; the evidence is sufficient to reach a conclusion.

## Conclusion

The claim is partially true (the ban on charity involvement) but significantly misleads by fabricating the specific reason for the ban. The claim falsely asserts the Trump children "stole donations for children with cancer," when the actual findings involved self-dealing and misuse of funds for personal and political purposes. Additionally, the claim omits that the ban applies only to New York charities, not all charitable activity. Including the accurate context would create a substantially different impression. Therefore, this is a case of `cherry-picking`.

### Verdict: CHERRY_PICKING

### Justification

The claim is largely false and misleading. While Eric Trump, Donald J. Trump Jr., and Ivanka Trump were indeed barred fromserving on charities in New York state as part of a [November 2019 settlement](https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/donald-trump/judge-orders-trump-pay-2-million-misusing-his-foundation-n1078306) with the New York Attorney General's office, the ban applies only to New York charities—not a blanket lifetime ban on "ever operating a charity" as the claim states. More critically, the claim fabricates the reason for the ban: there is no evidence that donations for children with cancer were stolen. According to the [legal proceedings](https://www.japantimes.co.jp/news/2018/06/15/business/new-york-sues-trump-charity-self-dealing/), the actual misconduct involved the [Trump Foundation](https://www.cbsnews.com/pittsburgh/news/new-york-attorney-general-sues-trump-foundation/) engaging in "repeated and willful self-dealing," misusing funds for personal expenses (portraits, sports memorabilia), settling personal legal disputes, and making an impermissible $25,000 political donation to Florida AG Pam Bondi—not the theft of cancer charity donations as alleged.